[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a concise “check-list + code-snippets” you can follow to keep the two plugins you’re already using after you switch the core library from **Mapbox GL JS** → **MapLibre GL JS**.

---

## 1️⃣ General steps you already have to do first  

| What you already did (or will do) | Why it matters for the plugins |
|-----------------------------------|--------------------------------|
| **Swap the core package**<br>`npm uninstall mapbox-gl && npm install maplibre-gl` | The plugins import `mapbox-gl` (or expect the global `mapboxgl` namespace). After the swap you must make the same namespace available under the new name. |
| **Replace the namespace**<br>`import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` | All plugin code that does `new mapboxgl.Marker()` etc. now needs `maplibregl`. |
| **Remove the access token** | Neither MapLibre nor the open-source plugins need a Mapbox token. If you keep a token in the code the plugins will still try to call Mapbox services (geocoder, tiles, sprites) and you’ll get 401 errors. |
| **Use a non-Mapbox style / source** (e.g. OpenFreeMap, OSM-based vector tiles) | The plugins themselves don’t care about the style, but the style must provide the sprite and glyph URLs that the plugins reference (e.g. the default draw icons). If you keep a `mapbox://` sprite you’ll see “Failed to load sprite” errors. |

> **Tip:** After you finish the core migration, run the app **without any plugins** first. Make sure the map loads, the style renders, and you can `map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl())`. Once the bare map works, add the plugins back one-by-one.

---

## 2️⃣ `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw`

### 2.1 Does it work out-of-the-box?  

Yes – **`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw` works with MapLibre** because it only uses the public MapLibre API (the same as Mapbox GL JS v1). The only things you have to adjust are:

| Item | What to change |
|------|----------------|
| **Import** | Keep the same import, but make sure the `mapboxgl` global points to `maplibregl`. The easiest way is to assign it once after you import MapLibre: <br>`import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';`<br>`(window as any).mapboxgl = maplibregl;` |
| **CSS class prefix** | The default CSS shipped with the plugin uses the `mapboxgl-` prefix (e.g. `.mapboxgl-ctrl-draw-btn`). MapLibre’s controls use the `maplibregl-` prefix, so the draw control will look unstyled unless you either: <br>1️⃣ **Override the CSS** to target the existing `mapboxgl-` classes (the simplest fix). <br>2️⃣ **Patch the plugin’s CSS** to use the `maplibregl-` prefix (more work, but keeps naming consistent). |
| **Sprite / icons** | The draw plugin loads its own sprite (`draw_sprite.png` + `draw_sprite.json`) from the package’s `dist/` folder. That sprite is **independent of Mapbox**, so you can keep it. Just make sure the sprite URL is reachable (the default works when you import the CSS). If you use a custom style that overrides the default sprite, you may need to add the draw sprite to your style’s `sprite` URL or load it manually with `map.addImage`. |

### 2.2 Minimal working example

```tsx
// index.ts (or wherever you bootstrap the map)
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

// Make the global name that the draw plugin expects
// (only needed if you keep the original @mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw import)
;(window as any).mapboxgl = maplibregl;

import MapboxDraw from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw';
import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw/dist/mapbox-gl-draw.css'; // <-- keep the CSS

// ---- create the map -------------------------------------------------
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12,
});

// ---- add the draw control -------------------------------------------
const draw = new MapboxDraw({
  displayControlsDefault: false,
  controls: {
    polygon: true,
    line_string: true,
    point: true,
    trash: true,
  },
});
map.addControl(draw);

// optional: fix the CSS prefix if you want MapLibre-styled controls
// (you can copy the CSS from the repo and replace `.mapboxgl-` → `.maplibregl-`)
// or just add a tiny rule:
const style = document.createElement('style');
style.innerHTML = `
.mapboxgl-ctrl-draw-btn { /* same as MapLibre control size */ }
`;
document.head.appendChild(style);
```

### 2.3 Alternative: Terra Draw (MapLibre-native)

If you prefer a plugin that ships **with the correct `maplibregl-` CSS** and is actively maintained for MapLibre, try **Terra Draw**:

```bash
npm install terra-draw terra-draw-maplibre-gl-adapter
```

```tsx
import { MapLibreGlAdapter } from 'terra-draw-maplibre-gl-adapter';
import { Draw } from 'terra-draw';

const draw = new Draw({
  adapter: new MapLibreGlAdapter(),
  // same options as mapbox-gl-draw
});
map.addControl(draw);
```

Both approaches are fine; keep the one you already know, or switch to Terra Draw for a “future-proof” dependency.

---

## 3️⃣ `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`

### 3.1 Why you *must* replace it  

`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` is tightly coupled to the **Mapbox Geocoding API** – it automatically appends your `accessToken` to every request and expects the Mapbox response shape. After you drop the token, the requests will be rejected (401) and the UI will show “No results”.

### 3.2 Recommended drop-in replacement  

**`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`** – a fork that works with any geocoding service you point it at.

```bash
npm install @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder
```

#### 3.2.1 Using the public Nominatim demo (good for testing)

```tsx
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css';

const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // Nominatim public endpoint – **do NOT use in production** (rate-limited)
  url: 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search',
  // Nominatim returns `display_name` instead of `place_name`
  // the fork maps it automatically, but you can also set a custom formatter
  // if you need a different UI.
  placeholder: 'Search OSM…',
  // optional: limit results, language, etc.
  // see https://nominatim.org/release-docs/latest/api/Search/
});

map.addControl(geocoder);
```

#### 3.2.2 Production-ready options

| Service | npm package (if any) | How to wire it to the geocoder |
|---------|----------------------|--------------------------------|
| **MapTiler Geocoding** | `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` (same package) | `url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/{query}.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY'` |
| **OpenCage** | same package | `url: 'https://api.opencagedata.com/geocode/v1/json?key=YOUR_KEY&q={query}'` |
| **Photon** (Komoot) | same package | `url: 'https://photon.komoot.io/api/?q={query}'` |
| **Pelias** (self-hosted) | same package | `url: 'https://YOUR_PELIAS_HOST/search?text={query}'` |

All of those services return a **GeoJSON FeatureCollection** (or a format that the fork can translate). If the response shape differs, you can provide a custom `transformResponse` function:

```tsx
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/{query}.json?key=YOUR_KEY',
  transformResponse: (response) => {
    // MapTiler already returns GeoJSON, so just return it.
    return response;
  },
});
```

### 3.3 Minimal working example (Nominatim)

```tsx
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12,
});

const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  url: 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search',
  placeholder: 'Search OSM…',
  // Nominatim requires a `format=json` query param; the fork adds it automatically.
  // If you need extra params:
  // params: { format: 'json', addressdetails: 1, limit: 5 },
});

map.addControl(geocoder);
```

### 3.4 CSS class rename (optional)

The original Mapbox geocoder CSS uses `.mapboxgl-ctrl-geocoder`. After the swap you’ll see the same class name because the fork keeps the original CSS. It works fine with MapLibre, but if you want a fully consistent naming scheme you can:

```css
.mapboxgl-ctrl-geocoder {
  /* same rules as .maplibregl-ctrl-geocoder */
}
```

Or copy the CSS from the repo and replace the prefix manually.

