## 4️⃣ Quick “cheat-sheet” summary

| Plugin | Keep / Replace | What to change |
|--------|----------------|----------------|
| **`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw`** | **Keep** (works) | 1️⃣ Assign `window.mapboxgl = maplibregl` **or** import the plugin after the MapLibre import.<br>2️⃣ Ensure the draw CSS is loaded; optionally rename `.mapboxgl-` → `.maplibregl-` for visual consistency.<br>3️⃣ No token needed. |
| **`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`** | **Replace** with `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` | 1️⃣ Install the MapLibre fork.<br>2️⃣ Point `url` to a non-Mapbox geocoder (Nominatim, MapTiler, etc.).<br>3️⃣ Remove any `accessToken` usage.<br>4️⃣ Load the new CSS (`maplibre-gl-geocoder.css`). |

---

## 5️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to debug them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Geocoder UI appears but no results** (or “Network error”) | Still hitting `

**What’s happening**

`map.loadImage` changed in **MapLibre GL JS v4.0.0**:

* In Mapbox GL JS (and MapLibre v1-v3) the method accepted a **callback**:  

  ```js
  map.loadImage(url, (err, image) => { … })
  ```

* Starting with **MapLibre v4** the method **returns a Promise** and **no longer accepts a callback**.  
  If you still pass a function, the library silently ignores it – that’s why you see the console warning *“callback passed to map.loadImage is not being called”* and the image never gets added, so your custom marker icons disappear.

---

## 1️⃣ Fix the `loadImage` call

### a) Using `async/await`

```js
// make sure the map is already loaded
map.on('load', async () => {
  try {
    const { data: img } = await map.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png');
    // `img` is an ImageBitmap (or HTMLImageElement in older browsers)
    map.addImage('pin', img);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load marker image', err);
  }
});
```

### b) Using `.then() / .catch()`

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  map.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png')
    .then(({ data: img }) => map.addImage('pin', img))
    .catch(err => console.error('Failed to load marker image', err));
});
```

### c) If you need to stay on the **1.x compatibility branch**

MapLibre still ships a `1.x` branch that behaves like the old Mapbox API.  
```bash
npm install maplibre-gl@1   # drop-in for v1-v3 behaviour
```
*Only use this as a temporary stop-gap* – the 1.x branch will not receive new features or security fixes.

---

## 2️⃣ Why the markers disappear (beyond the callback)

Even after fixing `loadImage`, a missing icon can be caused by any of the following:

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|--------|--------------|-----|
| **Marker shows the default “pin” (or nothing)** | `map.addImage` never ran (callback issue) | Fix `loadImage` as above. |
| **Marker shows a blank square** | Image was added **before** the map’s style was fully loaded, or the image is added to a **different style** (e.g., you change `map.setStyle` later). | Add the image **after** the `load` event *or* re-add it after every `styledata`/`style.load` event. |
| **Marker disappears after a style change** | The custom image lives only in the current style’s sprite. Changing the style clears it. | Re-add the image each time you call `map.setStyle`, or use a **persistent sprite** (see the “Sprites & icons” section below). |
| **CORS error in the console** | The image URL is on a domain that blocks cross-origin requests. | Serve the image with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` or host it on the same origin. |
| **`map.addImage` throws “Image with id 'pin' already exists”** | You’re adding the same image twice (e.g., after a style reload). | Guard with `if (!map.hasImage('pin')) { … }` or call `map.removeImage('pin')` first. |

---

## 3️⃣ Quick checklist for custom marker icons after a Mapbox → MapLibre migration

1. **Load the image with a Promise** (`await map.loadImage(...)` or `.then()`).
2. **Add the image after the map’s `load` event** (or after any `styledata` event if you swap styles later).
3. **Use the image name in your `SymbolLayer`**:

   ```js
   map.addLayer({
     id: 'my-pins',
     type: 'symbol',
     source: 'my-geojson',
     layout: {
       'icon-image': 'pin',   // matches the id you passed to addImage
       'icon-size': 0.8
     }
   });
   ```

4. **If you change the style at runtime**, re-add the image (or use a sprite URL that already contains the icon). Example:

   ```js
   map.on('styledata', () => {
     if (!map.hasImage('pin')) {
       map.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png')
         .then(({ data }) => map.addImage('pin', data));
     }
   });
   ```

5. **Check CORS** – the image must be fetchable without a cross-origin block.

---

## 4️⃣ Optional: Use a sprite instead of `loadImage`

If you have many icons, it’s more efficient to bundle them into a **sprite sheet** and reference them via `icon-image`. MapLibre can read a sprite URL defined in the style JSON:

```json
{
  "sprite": "https://my.cdn.com/sprites/sprite",
  "glyphs": "https://my.cdn.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  ...
}
```

*Advantages*  

* No runtime `loadImage` calls.  
* Works automatically across style changes.  

*How to create a sprite*  

1. Put your PNG icons in a folder.  
2. Run `npm i -g @mapbox/spritezero-cli` (or use the online tool).  
3. `spritezero my-sprite ./icons` → generates `my-sprite.png` and `my-sprite.json`.  
4. Host those files and set the `sprite` property in your style.

If you already have a Mapbox style JSON, you can **export it**, replace the `sprite` URL with your own, and keep the same `icon-image` names.

---

## 5️⃣ TL;DR – Minimal code change

```js
map.on('load', async () => {
  try {
    const { data: img } = await map.loadImage(
      'https://example.com/pin.png'   // <-- your icon URL
    );
    if (!map.hasImage('pin')) {
      map.addImage('pin', img);
    }
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Icon load failed', e);
  }
});
```

That single change (promise-based `loadImage`) will make the callback warning disappear and restore your custom marker icons.

---

### Need more help?

*If you still see blank markers after applying the fix, let me know:*  

* the exact MapLibre version you’re using (`npm list maplibre-gl`),  
* whether you ever call `map.setStyle` after the initial load,  
* and the URL of the image you’re trying to load (so I can check CORS headers).

I can then walk you through re-adding the image on style changes or switching to a sprite-based approach. Happy mapping!

Below are the two things that will break when you run the same code on **MapLibre GL JS v6** (the current “latest” release) and what you need to change.

---

## 1️⃣ `preserveDrawingBuffer` / `antialias` (and the other canvas flags)

**What Mapbox GL JS v2 allowed**

```js
new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/...',   // …
  preserveDrawingBuffer: true,
  antialias: true,
  // …
});
```

**Why it fails in MapLibre v6**

Starting with MapLibre v5 the canvas-context flags are no longer top-level `Map` options. They must be passed inside the `canvasContextAttributes` object. If you leave them where they are, MapLibre silently ignores them and you’ll see a warning in the console (or the map may render with the default values).

**Fix**

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
  canvasContextAttributes: {
    preserveDrawingBuffer: true,   // keep the canvas after a screenshot, etc.
    antialias: true                // smoother edges on vector tiles
    // you can also set: failIfMajorPerformanceCaveat, desynchronized, etc.
  },
  // other options (center, zoom, …) stay the same
});
```

> **Tip:** If you also need `failIfMajorPerformanceCaveat` or `desynchronized`, put them in the same object.

---

## 2️⃣ Chaining `map.on(...)` calls

**What Mapbox GL JS v2 allowed**

```js
map.on('load', onLoad)
   .on('click', 'my-layer', onClick);
```

`map.on` returned the map instance, so you could chain as many listeners as you wanted.

**Why it breaks in MapLibre v6**

From MapLibre v5 onward `map.on` returns a **subscription object** (the value you can later call `.remove()` on). Because the return type changed, the chain stops after the first call – the second `.on` is being invoked on the subscription, not on the map, and you’ll get a runtime error like:

```
TypeError: map.on(...).on is not a function
```

**Fix – call `on` separately for each event**

```js
map.on('load', onLoad);
map.on('click', 'my-layer', onClick);
```

If you like a one-liner, you can wrap it in a helper:

```js
function addListeners(m, listeners) {
  listeners.forEach(([event, ...args]) => m.on(event, ...args));
  return m;
}

// usage
addListeners(map, [
  ['load', onLoad],
  ['click', 'my-layer', onClick]
]);
```

But the simplest and most future-proof approach is the two explicit calls shown first.

---

## Quick “migration checklist” for the rest of the app

| ✅ Item | What to do (if you haven’t already) |
|--------|--------------------------------------|
| **Package & import** | `npm uninstall mapbox-gl && npm install maplibre-gl`  <br> `import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';` (or `import {Map} from 'maplibre-gl';`) |
| **Namespace** | Replace every `mapboxgl` with `maplibregl`. |
| **Access token** | Remove `mapboxgl.accessToken = '…'`. |
| **Style URL** | Use a non-`mapbox://` style (e.g. OpenFreeMap, your own TileJSON, or a MapTiler style). |
| **CSS class names** | Change `mapboxgl-` → `maplibregl-` if you reference them in custom CSS. |
| **Callbacks → Promises** | `map.loadImage`, `source.getCluster*`, `map.setRTLTextPlugin` now return promises. Convert `await` or `.then()` instead of passing a callback. |
| **v2-only APIs** | `setFog` → `setSky`; free-camera methods → `calculateCameraOptionsFromCameraLngLatAltRotation` + `jumpTo/easeTo/flyTo`. |
| **Plugins** | • `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw` works after you rename its CSS constants (or switch to `terra-draw`). <br> • `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-compare` → `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-compare`. <br> • Replace any Mapbox-hosted services (Geocoding, Directions, etc.) with open equivalents (`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`, OSRM, etc.). |
| **WebGL2 requirement** | MapLibre v6 requires WebGL2. Test on your target browsers; older browsers will now throw a `GPUInitializationError`. |
| **Other breaking changes** | * Chained `on` → separate calls (covered above). <br> * `antialias`, `preserveDrawingBuffer`, `failIfMajorPerformanceCaveat` → `canvasContextAttributes`. <br> * `JSON.parse(feature.properties.x)` – nested properties are now real objects, so you usually don’t need to parse. |

---

### TL;DR for the two items you asked about

```js
// 1️⃣ Canvas flags
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
  canvasContextAttributes: {
    preserveDrawingBuffer: true,
    antialias: true
  },
  // …other options
});

// 2️⃣ Event listeners (no chaining)
map.on('load', onLoad);
map.on('click', 'my-layer', onClick);
```

Apply the rest of the checklist and your Mapbox v2 code should run unchanged on the latest MapLibre GL JS. If you hit any other “blank map”, “401”, or “method not found” errors, refer back to the migration guide sections on **style URLs**, **access token removal**, and **v2-only APIs**. Happy mapping!
